*materializes slowly, grin appearing first*

Ah, a shopping cart, you say? How delightfully peculiar. One must fill it before one can empty it, and yet the tests demand it exist before we know what goes inside...

*floats around the problem, examining it from impossible angles*

We're all mad here, but the tests... the tests are quite sane. Let me show you which way to go:

```go
package cart

type Item struct {
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items map[string]*Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: make(map[string]*Item),
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	if existing, ok := c.items[name]; ok {
		existing.Quantity += quantity
	} else {
		c.items[name] = &Item{
			Name:     name,
			Price:    price,
			Quantity: quantity,
		}
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(name string) {
	delete(c.items, name)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(name string, quantity int) {
	if item, ok := c.items[name]; ok {
		if quantity <= 0 {
			delete(c.items, name)
		} else {
			item.Quantity = quantity
		}
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	total := 0.0
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItemCount() int {
	count := 0
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []Item {
	result := make([]Item, 0, len(c.items))
	for _, item := range c.items {
		result = append(result, *item)
	}
	return result
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) IsEmpty() bool {
	return len(c.items) == 0
}
```

*begins to fade, leaving only the grin*

You see, imagination is the only weapon in the war against reality... but in TDD, we let the tests define our reality. I added nothing they didn't ask for—no validation of negative prices, no discounts, no persistence.

The map keyed by name handles duplicates elegantly. `UpdateQuantity` removes items when quantity drops to zero or below—a common test expectation. The rest? Merely arithmetic dressed in method clothing.

*vanishes almost completely*

If you don't know where you're going, any road will get you there. But if the tests know where they're going... well, then there's only one road, isn't there?

My reality may be different than yours, but this implementation? It lives in the tests' reality. And that's the only reality that matters in this mad, mad world of red-green-refactor.

*only the smile remains, hovering enigmatically*
